# ── Line endings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This project was developed on Windows, and the tree arrived at 113 LF files and 91
# CRLF files. Without normalisation, every Linux contributor sees 91 files as wholly
# rewritten and every diff is unreadable.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores LF in the repository and checks out LF everywhere. Git
# handles the conversion; nothing in the toolchain cares.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell keeps CRLF on checkout. It runs fine with LF, but Windows tooling and
# editors expect CRLF for .ps1, and this is the file a Windows user opens first.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Explicitly binary, so git never tries a line-ending or diff pass on them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.onnx binary
*.7z   binary
*.zip  binary

# ── Generated artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are *outputs*, produced by `make schema`, `make ingest`, `make ablate` and the
# eval runner. Marking them tells GitHub two useful truths:
#
#   - collapse them in pull-request diffs, because reviewing a regenerated 400KB JSONL
#     line by line is noise
#   - leave them out of the language statistics
#
# The second matters more than it looks. By bytes this repository is 54% JSON/JSONL and
# 29% Python, so GitHub's language bar would announce it as a JSON project. It is a
# Python project that happens to publish its measurements, and the header should say so.
evals/testsets/generated.jsonl   linguist-generated=true
evals/golden/derived.jsonl       linguist-generated=true
reports/*.jsonl                  linguist-generated=true
web/src/demo/traces/*.json       linguist-generated=true
web/src/lib/trace.schema.json    linguist-generated=true

# Reports are prose about measurements and should stay visible in diffs — a changed
# number there is exactly what a reviewer wants to see. Only the raw JSONL is collapsed.
reports/*.md                     -linguist-generated
